# Line endings
#
# Why this file exists:
#   Git's default on Windows (core.autocrlf=true) checks out .sh files with
#   CRLF, and a CRLF shell script dies on the *first* line it executes:
#
#     line 13: set: -: invalid option
#     line 16: $'\r': command not found
#     line 17: cd: $'...CommandCapture\r': No such file or directory
#
#   It never runs a single assertion -- it just dies before producing any
#   output, so it looks like "the test suite failed" rather than "the file
#   has the wrong line endings".
#
#   We hit exactly that: a Python edit script rewrote a .sh file with the
#   platform default separator, which on Windows is CRLF.
#
# So the line endings are pinned per file type instead of relying on a
# global setting.

# ── Shell scripts: always LF, on every platform ───────────────────────
*.sh text eol=lf

# ── Sources: LF in the repository, native on checkout ─────────────────
*.c      text
*.h      text
*.cpp    text
*.hpp    text
*.py     text
*.md     text
*.txt    text
*.json   text
*.yml    text
*.yaml   text

# ── Windows-native scripts and projects: CRLF ─────────────────────────
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.ps1    text eol=crlf
*.csproj text eol=crlf
*.proj   text eol=crlf
*.sln    text eol=crlf
Makefile text eol=lf

# ── Binary: never touched ─────────────────────────────────────────────
*.exe    binary
*.dll    binary
*.so     binary
*.lib    binary
*.a      binary
*.obj    binary
*.o      binary
*.pdb    binary
*.ilk    binary
